                <pb facs="0068" n="50" sameAs="frus:frus1946v02/pg50"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Council/4–1046: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-10T19:00:00-05:00">April 10, 1946—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1614. For Caffery from the Secretary. In reply to Bidault letter April 9
                    regarding meeting of the Council of Foreign Ministers in Paris on April 25, you
                    might suggest that the French Government send invitations to the participating
                    governments, that is the governments concerned with the preparation of the
                    treaties in question. The French Government might also wish as a matter of
                    courtesy to notify China of the meetings although China would not participate
                    unless German questions are added to agenda. We are prepared to have German
                    questions raised in the French communication of March 1 added to the agenda with
                    the understanding that they will be taken up after Council has completed its
                    work on draft treaties.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
